answers.body:
As stated in the comments, OpenMP tasks are more suited for this kind of algorithm. You just have to modify the ParallelQuickSort routine: void ParallelQuickSort (vector<int> &dataList,  int nLower, int nUpper)                                                                                             
{
    if (nLower < nUpper)
    {
        int nSplit = partition (dataList, nLower, nUpper);
        if (nUpper - nLower >= 128) {
            #pragma omp task default(shared)
            ParallelQuickSort (dataList, nLower, nSplit - 1);
        } else {
            ParallelQuickSort (dataList, nLower, nSplit - 1);
        }
        ParallelQuickSort (dataList, nSplit + 1, nUpper);
        #pragma omp taskwait
    }
} Comments: Once the segment is partitioned, just one additional task needs to be created for one of the partitions, while the other one can processed by the current task You need a threshold to decide if a new task has to be created or not, because of 2 reasons: the task creation overhead can hide any parallelization benefit for small partitions with a small segment to be sorted, the 2 partitions likely belong to the same cache line (64 or 128 bytes), and synchronizing the cache lines between different cores is very costly. You have to try various threshold values (I've put an arbitrary 128 elements in the code) to see which one is optimum.

