answers.body:
Because of Python's "Global Interpreter Lock", only one thread can run Python code at a time. However, other threads can do I/O at the same time. If you want to allow other threads to run (just like I/O does) you can surround your code with these macros: Py_BEGIN_ALLOW_THREADS

// computation goes here

Py_END_ALLOW_THREADS Other Python threads will be allowed to run while the computation is happening. You can't access anything from Python between these two lines - so get that data in order before Py_BEGIN_ALLOW_THREADS . Reference

