In the code that I am writing, I have two functions: Residue(u,f,h) and Relax(u,f,h) @cython.cdivision(True)
@cython.boundscheck(False)
@cython.wraparound(False)
@cython.profile(True)
cdef double[:,:] Residue(double[:,:] u, double[:,:] f, double h):
    cdef:
        Py_ssize_t i, j
        Py_ssize_t N = u.shape[0]
        double h2 = h*h
        double[:,:] res = np.zeros([N,N], dtype=DTYPE)

    for i in range(1,N-1):
        for j in range(1,N-1):
            res[i,j] = f[i,j] - (u[i-1,j] + u[i+1,j] + u[i,j-1] + u[i,j+1] \
            - 4*u[i,j])/h2
    return res

@cython.boundscheck(False)
@cython.wraparound(False)
@cython.profile(True)
cdef void Relax(double[:,:] u, double[:,:] f, double h):
    cdef:
        Py_ssize_t i, j, N
        double h2 = h*h 
    N = u.shape[0]
    for i in range(1,N-1):
        for j in range(1,N-1):
            u[i,j] = (u[i-1,j] + u[i+1,j] + u[i,j-1] + u[i,j+1] \
            - h2*f[i,j])/4 How they interact is that at some point, the output of Residue gets inputted as f inside Relax . These two functions are called multiple times during the runtime of the code. Using cProfile , when I parallelize Residue by using prange on the outermost for loop, I see that the function gains a modest speed-up compared to the serial code (as expected). However, unexpectedly, the entire code takes longer to finish. I have identified where it slows down: Relax , which has remained unchanged. For some reason, whatever gains I get from parallelizing Residue gets drowned by how much Relax gets slowed down. All other functions seem unaffected when I parallelize Residue . For example: Serial code runtime: 14.840 seconds ncalls tottime function 5512 10.618 Relax 743 1.799 Residue Parallel code runtime: 17.019 seconds (+2.179) ncalls tottime function 5512 12.745 (+2.127) Relax 743 1.622 (-0.177) Residue As one can see, most of the slow-down is due to Relax . I can't seem to identify why this is so, when the only thing that has changed is Residue . I kind of suspect there might be some kind of variable type conversion that might be occuring once I use prange.

