I have a function that computes the n-th power of a matrix using power. The code is written in C: void matrix_power(matrix *result, matrix *mat, int pow) {
    if (pow == 0) {
        for (int r = 0; r < mat->rows; r++) {
            for (int c = 0; c < mat->cols; c++) {
                result->content[r][c] = r == c;
            }
        }
    } else if (pow == 1) {
        for (int r = 0; r < mat->rows; r++) {
            for (int c = 0; c < mat->cols; c++) {
                result->content[r][c] = mat->content[r][c];
            }
        }
    } else {
        matrix *temp;
        copy_matrix(temp, mat); //now temp becomes mat
        matrix_power(temp, mat, pow /= 2);
        matrix_multiply(result, temp, temp);
        if (pow % 2 == 1) {
            copy_matrix(temp, result);
            matrix_multiply(result, temp, mat);
        }
        deallocate_matrix(temp);
    }
} I want to optimize my code so it's 1000 times faster than the naive implementation of matrix powering. Currently my implementation is only 300 times faster. My matrix_multiply function has been decently optimized using SIMD and openMP. I want to use openMP to achieve better parallel performance. How can I do that? Or is there any other methods I should consider to reap a significant speed boost?

